# BT-13 DraftReplay — v7.3 BUILD SPEC (FINAL)
BT-13 Writing Process Replay · Level 3 Advanced · BIOS V2 School Buildathon · TIET Patiala

ROLE: Build "DraftReplay" — a writing-process transparency tool. It is NOT a plagiarism
checker and NOT an AI detector. It records how a document grows and lets a reviewer
replay it, beside the student's own AI-use declaration.

STACK (fixed):
- Next.js (App Router) + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS
- Next.js route handlers for all API access
- Supabase Postgres (free tier) via node-postgres (pg) Pool. CONNECTION STRING = the
  SESSION pooler (IPv4-capable). Direct database hosts may be IPv6-only and refused on
  venue networks; the transaction-mode pooler (port 6543) adds pooler-mode behaviour we
  refuse to debug on build day. Verify with a real authenticated append at 08:45 on
  31 Oct, through this exact string.
- Pool sizing: new Pool({ connectionString, max: 2, idleTimeoutMillis: 10_000 }) —
  a small cap; serverless auto-scaling must not exhaust the database connection limit.
- Local cache in IndexedDB for unsent snapshots and the write key
- Dark, clean, warm UI. Big fonts. One accent color (emerald). Simple English copy.

HARD RULES (never violate):
1. NEVER capture keystrokes. Snapshots only: the full text at moments of change.
2. Paste is labelled ONLY from real paste signals (inputType "insertFromPaste" and/or
   the paste event's clipboardData). Autocorrect, voice input, suggestions, IME
   composition, and drag-and-drop are NOT pastes.
3. No personal data. No names, emails, accounts, logins. Document IDs: 128+ bits random.
   No IP, user agent or device ID stored by the app.
4. No scores, verdicts, "AI %" or any label that judges a person. The declaration sits
   beside the evidence as an equal.
5. Every seeded/demo document shows a persistent "FICTIONAL DEMO DOCUMENT" label.
6. The SERVER enforces security, not the UI. RLS on every table (blocks anonymous
   database access — see AUTHORISATION MODEL); the DATABASE CONNECTION STRING lives
   only in server env; wrong/missing keys rejected server-side (401/403).
7. Local cache near ~4 MB -> visible warning. NEVER drop snapshots silently.
8. The write key never appears in any URL, query string, exported bundle, or server log.

FIXED DECISIONS:
D1  Backend version storage is REQUIRED ("a backend that stores versions"). Built before
    the 13:00 checkpoint. If it fails, ship the local fallback AND disclose exactly what
    is not server-stored — never present the fallback as meeting the spec.
D2  One duplicate rule: never save a snapshot whose text equals the last saved text —
    regardless of event type.
D3  Snapshot ordering: client-generated snapshotId, SERVER-assigned seq, gap-free,
    idempotent retries.
D4  Paste counts come from the clipboard (exact). Diff-derived counts are ESTIMATES and
    are labelled as such. Counts use JavaScript UTF-16 code units (.length).
D5  Offline: editing + ordered queued sync is REQUIRED. Offline RELOAD (service worker)
    is STRETCH and must not be claimed unless tested. These are different capabilities.
D6  Two tabs: single-author MVP; a second tab shows "This document is open elsewhere".
    No merge. Stated as a limit.
D7  No hash chains. No signatures. Honesty labels carry the trust story.
D8  Classification is BEST-EFFORT; saved states are truth. Budget <= 45 minutes total on
    the classifier. Reliable saved states matter more than perfect labels.
D9  Drag-and-drop text (insertFromDrop) is recorded as TYPING (diff-estimate). No block
    card for drops. We may miss that block boundary; we never falsely add one.

DATA MODEL:
Document {
  docId: string        // 22+ chars base64url, client-generated, 128+ bits
  title: string        // content, not identity
  createdAt: timestamp
  lastSeq: number      // server-managed
  version: number      // starts 1; +1 after a declaration is saved
  keyHash: string      // SHA-256 of the write key (server never sees the plain key)
  declaration: { level: "none"|"ideas"|"drafted"|"wrote-parts", note: string(<=140), declaredAt }
}
Snapshot {
  snapshotId: string   // client UUID (crypto.randomUUID) — idempotency key
  seq: number          // SERVER-assigned, gap-free per document
  t: timestamp         // SERVER time (authoritative)
  clientT: timestamp   // display only; source of all time/span display
  text: string         // FULL text at this moment
  eventType: "paste" | "large_delete" | "undo" | "pause"
  inserted: number     // UTF-16 code units; exact for paste, else estimate
  deleted: number      // estimate unless measured at selection level
  countSource: "event" | "diff-estimate"
}
WriteKey: 32 random bytes (crypto.getRandomValues) base64url, generated in the BROWSER
at document creation. Server stores only its SHA-256 hash. Sent per write in an
Authorization header over HTTPS. Stored in IndexedDB only.

Supabase tables:
docs(id, title, created_at, last_seq, version, key_hash, declaration jsonb)
snapshots(doc_id, snapshot_id, seq, t, client_t, text, event_type, inserted, deleted,
          count_source)  UNIQUE (doc_id, snapshot_id)
shares(token_hash, doc_id, created_at, revoked_at)

CAPTURE LOGIC:
Two diff baselines:
- lastInputText: updated every input event; classifies the single action just performed.
- lastSnapshotText: updated only when a snapshot is saved; used for replay + dedupe.

Per input event:
1. before = lastInputText; after = editor.value; compute common prefix p and suffix s
   (p + s <= min(len(before), len(after))); inserted = len(after)-p-s;
   deleted = len(before)-p-s; lastInputText = after.
2. Paste path (event-first): if inputType starts with "insertFromPaste" -> eventType
   "paste"; inserted = clipboard text length from the paste event (countSource "event")
   when a non-empty text/plain clipboard value exists, else diff (countSource
   "diff-estimate"). Snapshot IMMEDIATELY.
3. Undo path: inputType "historyUndo"/"historyRedo" -> eventType "undo". Snapshot
   IMMEDIATELY. Never label an undo as a deletion or a rewrite.
4. Large delete / replace path: deleted > 40 -> eventType "large_delete". Snapshot
   IMMEDIATELY. (Select-and-type lands here with both counts recorded.)
5. insertFromDrop (drag-and-drop): recorded as TYPING (diff-estimate). No block card.
6. Otherwise -> "typing": no immediate snapshot; starts/refreshes the 2 s pause timer.
7. Pause: 2 s with no input -> snapshot (eventType "pause"). Any immediate snapshot
   resets the pause timer.
DEDUPE (D2): before saving ANY snapshot, if text === lastSnapshotText -> skip and drop.
Initialise lastSnapshotText = "".
Every snapshot stores the FULL text.
Ambiguity guard (D8): repeated characters, composition, exotic input -> save with the
best-guess label and countSource "diff-estimate". Never block saving to perfect a label.

CAPTURE TESTS (record pass/fail):
T1  Type 3 sentences, wait 5 s -> one "pause" snapshot
T2  Type without pausing, delete a 12-char word -> "typing"; NO large_delete; snapshot
    only on pause
T3  Delete 60 chars in one action -> "large_delete", deleted = 60 (not cumulative)
T4  Paste over a selection (pasted text overlaps surrounding chars) -> "paste";
    inserted = clipboard length (exact); snapshot saved even if diff math is odd
T5  Undo after a large delete -> "undo" snapshot; no duplicate row (texts differ -> both)
T6  Pause, then pause again with no change -> one snapshot only (D2)
T7  IME composition / autocorrect / voice input -> "typing", NEVER "paste"
T7b Drag-and-drop text -> "typing"; no block card; counts marked diff-estimate

API CONTRACT (all size-validated, all authorization server-side):
POST   /api/docs                 create {title} + browser write key (server receives the
                                 HASH only) -> {docId}
POST   /api/docs/:id/snapshots   append snapshot; auth: Authorization header (write key)
POST   /api/docs/:id/declaration store declaration; version +1; same auth
GET    /api/docs/:id             read: write key (Authorization header only — NEVER in a
                                 query string) OR valid reviewer token
GET    /api/docs/:id/meta        SAME auth as GET /api/docs/:id — write key OR valid
                                 reviewer token (?token=..., reviewer links only). Each
                                 route verifies the key/token belongs to THAT document
                                 id. Returns metadata for ALL snapshots (seq, t,
                                 eventType, counts) PLUS captureSpanMs, captureStartMs,
                                 captureEndMs (derived SERVER-side from stored clientT)
                                 — the reviewer screen never receives raw clientT
GET    /api/docs/:id/text?from=  SAME auth as above (write key OR valid reviewer token).
                                 Snapshot text chunk, 50 at a time, oldest first,
                                 prefetch the next chunk before the scrubber reaches it
POST   /api/docs/:id/share       create share token -> {shareUrl}
POST   /api/docs/:id/revoke      revoke a share token
DELETE /api/docs/:id             delete doc + versions (write key)

- The reviewer token is accepted ONLY by the three GET routes. No other route accepts
  it. The write key never travels in a query string. /meta and /text MUST accept the
  reviewer token — the reviewer screen reads the span from /meta and the text from /text.
- Export is CLIENT-SIDE, built from the local cache. There is no server export route
  and none shall be built.

APPEND RULES (normative — these are part of the spec, not suggestions):
a) Authenticate the write key and validate the payload BEFORE touching the pool.
   A rejected request must never take the docs row lock.
b) The append runs as ONE transaction: UPDATE (allocate seq) + INSERT. That transaction
   is what keeps seq gap-free.
c) The INSERT is a PLAIN insert. NEVER use ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING — a swallowed conflict
   commits the seq increment and creates a permanent gap.
d) On unique violation (SQLSTATE 23505): the transaction aborts and the increment rolls
   back with it. Catch it, ROLLBACK, SELECT the existing row, return it with 200.
e) Mechanism (i) only: node-postgres (pg) Pool, session-pooler connection, the code
   pattern in the reference block. Do NOT wrap the append in a PL/pgSQL function
   (COMMIT is invalid inside a function body). Do NOT mix styles.
f) Idempotency: same snapshotId -> return the stored {seq, t}, insert nothing. A
   timeout-then-retry never creates a second version.
g) ONE checked-out client for the ENTIRE transaction: pool.connect() -> BEGIN -> ... ->
   COMMIT/ROLLBACK -> release() in a finally block. NEVER run the statements through
   pool.query() separately — each call can land on a DIFFERENT pool connection and split
   the transaction, which produces a permanent seq gap and fails T9b.
h) CONNECTION STRING = Supabase SESSION pooler (IPv4-capable). Direct database hosts
   may be IPv6-only and refused on venue networks; the transaction-mode pooler (6543)
   adds pooler-mode behaviour we refuse to debug on build day. Verify with a real
   authenticated append at 08:45 on 31 Oct, through this exact string.

AUTHORISATION MODEL (read this twice):
Our server connects to Postgres as the TABLE OWNER, so RLS does not apply to our own
queries — the write-key check in the route handler IS the guard. RLS is enabled on
every table and blocks direct anonymous access to the database (T16, via the anon REST
key). Never rely on RLS to protect a route; always check the key or token in the handler.

ORDERING AND RETRIES:
- Client generates snapshotId (UUID) at capture; never changes across retries.
- Server assigns seq, gap-free, via APPEND RULES.
- Response returns {seq, t}; the client marks the snapshot Synced.
- Client queue (IndexedDB) sends pending snapshots in capture order; server seq is
  authoritative. UI shows "Synced / Pending: N" per document. Never silent.

LIMITS (server-enforced): snapshot text <= 32 KB; <= 2,000 snapshots/doc; body <= 1 MB.
Rate limit: per-document and per-write-key — the real, enforced limits — plus a
best-effort per-IP limit held IN MEMORY ONLY (never persisted, reset on deploy;
best-effort only on serverless). We do not store IP addresses. The limit must allow a
full offline queue to flush (>= 60 appends/min); on 429 the client backs off
exponentially and respects Retry-After. Over-limit -> 413/429 with a calm message.
On hitting the snapshot cap: block new snapshots with a clear message. Never drop
silently.

BACKEND TESTS (record pass/fail):
T8  Create -> append -> reload -> read back: versions in seq order with server timestamps
T9  Simulate timeout after the server stored a snapshot; retry same snapshotId ->
