SERVER-SIDE ENFORCEMENT CHECKLIST (a client-side check is not security):
1. EVERY document operation (read, append, declare, share, revoke, delete) is scoped to
   its document AND its authorization rule. Deliverable: a route x auth table in the
   code review. (Export is client-side — see API contract.)
2. Wrong/missing keys are rejected by the server (T11 via curl), never merely hidden.
3. Request payloads validated server-side (types, lengths, eventType whitelist) (T12).
4. Server errors and logs NEVER contain the write key, document text, or URLs carrying
   share tokens. Request headers are never logged. T15 verifies by triggering a write
   error and inspecting logs.
5. RLS blocks anonymous raw table reads (T16).
6. The server secret is the DATABASE CONNECTION STRING (session pooler), which grants
   table-owner access — MORE powerful than any API key. It lives only in server env:
   never in client code, never in the repo, never in a screenshot. If it ever touches a
   chat, screenshot or repo, RESET THE DATABASE PASSWORD.
7. Share tokens stored hashed, revocable, separate from the write key.
8. The "What we record" note (below, verbatim) tells the truth about what exists.
9. The write key is accepted ONLY from the Authorization header — never from a query
   string, body, or cookie.

OFFLINE:
- REQUIRED: with the network off, editing, capture and replay continue. Unsent snapshots
  queue in order (snapshotId preserved) in IndexedDB; on reconnect they sync exactly
  once, in order (T13). UI shows Pending/Synced.
- STRETCH (only if tested): offline reload via service worker.
- Replay reads the LOCAL STORE first; the server is a sync overlay. Replay must work
  with no network.
- T13: devtools offline -> 3 snapshots -> reconnect -> each stored exactly once, in
  order, seq gap-free; duplicate sends prove idempotent.

EXPORT / IMPORT (honesty labels, no cryptography):
- Export is CLIENT-SIDE, built from the local cache. There is no server export route.
- Export allow-list ONLY: title, version, export time, snapshots (seq, t, eventType,
  inserted, deleted, countSource, text), declaration, captureSpanMs, captureStartMs,
  captureEndMs (three integers computed at export time from clientT). NOTHING else.
  Raw clientT is NEVER exported. (T14: grep the file for the key string — absent.)
- IMPORT — SPAN FIELDS: captureSpanMs, captureStartMs, captureEndMs are OPTIONAL in the
  import schema; a bundle without them imports fine. If captureStartMs and captureEndMs
  are both present, RECOMPUTE the span from them (never trust the stored value);
  validate endMs >= startMs; if not, drop the span and show "No time span in this
  bundle." An imported span is an UNTRUSTED claim from the file, shown under the
  persistent "not independently verified" label — never presented as a measurement
  DraftReplay made.
- Import guard: validate JSON schema; enforce file/text size limits; NEVER auto-trust
  imported content. Schema-violating bundle -> rejected with a clear message (T17).
- Every imported replay displays, persistently, above the replay:
  "Imported replay — history not independently verified."
- Never describe bundles as tamper-proof, signed, or verified evidence.

SCREENS (build in this order):
1. WRITE — large text area (>=60% viewport), title field, live ticker in monospace:
   "Snapshot #N saved · P pastes · D deletions". On paste, a neutral amber toast flashes
   "Pasted as a block" (the only toast; it judges nothing).
   Buttons: "Finish & Declare", "Open Replay". A "Try it yourself" card (3 one-line
   steps) on first visit.
2. BACKEND ROUND-TRIP — create, append, read back with write-key auth (T8-T12, T9b).
   CHECKPOINT 13:00 — if this fails, stop and tell the team immediately.
3. REPLAY — slider (first -> latest) + Play (one snapshot per 350 ms). Text GROWS as you
   scrub. Event cards (the exact words judges will read):
   - "paste" -> amber glow + "Pasted as a block"
   - "large_delete" AND inserted > 0 -> "Replaced approx. {deleted} characters with {inserted}"
   - "large_delete" AND inserted = 0 -> "Removed approx. {deleted} characters"
   - "undo" -> "Undo"
   - "pause" -> text appears, no card
   Time strip below the slider: one bar per snapshot; BAR HEIGHT COMPUTED AT RENDER TIME
   from the diff between consecutive snapshot texts (never from stored inserted fields);
   paste = amber tick, large_delete = red tick.
   On-screen note (always visible): "Shows saved states, not every keystroke."
   Data: metadata for ALL snapshots up front (small); snapshot TEXT loads in chunks of 50,
   oldest first, PREFETCHED before the scrubber reaches them. Replay reads the local
   store first.
4. DECLARE — "Did you use AI help while writing this? This is not a test — honest
   answers are the point." Radios: "No AI help" / "AI for ideas only (brainstorm,
   structure)" / "AI helped draft some text" / "AI wrote parts of this". Optional note
   (<=140 chars). Label under the box: "Your self-declaration — not proof of how this
   was written." Saved with the document; later edits create version 2.
5. WHAT WE RECORD — display the following note VERBATIM. Do not rewrite, reword, shrink
   or regenerate it. Length rule: <= 150 words, excluding the title. The span feature
   adds NO sentence to this note (storage truth lives here; display truth lives in the
   span caveat). Copy-audit acceptance: every sentence must match what the code stores.

   --- BEGIN NOTE (copy exactly) ---
   Title: "What DraftReplay records and why"

   We record snapshots — the full document at moments of change — never keystrokes: we
   show effort, not surveillance. Paste lengths come from the clipboard and are exact;
   other counts are estimates. We keep no separate record of deleted text; earlier
   snapshots still show what the document said before. No names, no accounts. A document
   carries a random ID — not anonymity: the text can identify its author. Versions are
   stored on our server under that ID. Anyone with the write key can add versions; losing
   it stops server writes — no recovery — clearing this device's site data removes
   the local copy. The key holder can delete the document and its versions; nothing is
   deleted automatically. A reviewer sees a replay only when you share it; imported
   replays are labelled "not independently verified". Your declaration is self-reported.
   The rate limiter reads your IP in memory only — never saved.
   --- END NOTE ---

   Linked from every screen ("?" in the header).
6. REVIEWER REPLAY — open a share link (revocable token) or import a JSON bundle. Show:
   the replay, the declaration card BESIDE it (equal size), and a two-column card
   "What this shows / What this cannot tell you"
   Shows: how the text arrived over time; where blocks were pasted; what the student
   declared. Cannot tell: who actually typed; whether a paste was a quotation; intent;
   how long you actually spent — only the span between the first and last saved state is
   visible, and it comes from the student's own device clock.
   Imported bundles get the persistent "not independently verified" label.

TIME SPAN DISPLAY (derived display — an indicator, NOT evidence; build AFTER all core
screens; polish only):
- Span = clientT(last snapshot) - clientT(first snapshot).
  - Student's own screens: computed in the BROWSER from local clientT.
  - Reviewer screen: GET /api/docs/:id/meta returns captureSpanMs, captureStartMs,
    captureEndMs (derived SERVER-side from stored clientT). The reviewer screen never
    receives raw clientT.
  - NEVER compute from server t: offline snapshots all get the same server t at
    reconnect, so a server-t span would collapse to ~0.
- Display on REPLAY and REVIEWER ONLY (never WRITE — no stopwatch optics), two lines:
    Line 1: "Saved states span 2 h 00 m (12 saves)"
    Line 2: "First save 11:00 AM · Last save 1:00 PM"
  - Labels are "First save" / "Last save" — NOT "Started" / "Finished": the first save
    fires 2 s after the first input, not when the document was opened.
  - Times formatted in the VIEWER's local timezone, 12-hour with AM/PM. Do not store or
    export a timezone.
  - If the first/last save date differs from today, show the date beside the time
    (e.g. "First save 31 Oct, 11:00 AM"). Never show an ambiguous time.
  - One saved state: show "1 saved state — no span yet". Zero: "No saved states yet".
- Caveat, ALWAYS visible directly below the two lines (never a tooltip):
  "Measured from saved states on the student's device clock, not from activity. Time
  away, thinking, and reading a reference all look the same. This is an indicator,
  not proof."
- NEUTRAL LANGUAGE ONLY. No colour, no rating, no gap list, no largest-gap metric, no
  comparison between students, no "you only wrote 4 minutes" framing. (Rule 4.)
- Honest window rule: the span covers first-save to last-save. Within that window it
  counts every gap as time (so it is generous); the first stretch of typing before the
  first save falls outside it. The clock is the student's own device clock and cannot
  be verified — so the generosity holds only if the clock is honest. Never present the
  span as a measure of productivity.
- No numbered test (trivially derived); eyeball beside T19.

SEEDED DEMO (?demo=1) — "FICTIONAL DEMO DOCUMENT" labels on both. Both docs are
READ-ONLY (the editor is DISABLED for seeded docs; live typing in the demo always
happens on a fresh empty document — this protects the span story from live keystrokes).
TIME STORY (this is the part seeds get wrong):
- Both docs' clientT values are computed when ?demo=1 first generates them and are
  ALWAYS ANCHORED TO THE PAST: last save = generation time minus 8 minutes; first save
  = last save minus ~52 minutes. Clock times on the reviewer screen therefore always
  precede "now" — never future timestamps, whatever hour the pitch happens. NEVER seed
  uniform intervals.
- On demo morning, clear the demo cache (or regenerate seeds fresh) so the clock times
  are from demo morning — not build day.
- Hero doc "My Favourite Festival (Class 8 English)" (~12 saves, span 45-60 min):
  typing bursts 30-90 s apart during active stretches; TWO visibly longer gaps (one
  8-12 min before the rewrite, one ~25 min mid-essay break); polish edits near the end.
  Story beats: title + first sentence -> bursts -> 2-sentence pasted Diwali block (with
  an attribution inside the text) -> more typing -> ~300-char paragraph removed and
  rewritten -> polish -> declaration "AI for ideas only — used AI to brainstorm a list
  of festivals".
- "History Essay (fictional)": four pasted blocks; declaration "AI wrote parts of this";
  its own distinct ~20-minute time story so the two validation docs differ in time
  pattern as well as paste pattern.
- Eyeball check beside T19: the span line reads 45-60 min, the timeline shows at least
  two visibly long gaps, and every clock time is in the past.

